# November 22, 2026: a first scatter of places across Oregon, the state with the fewest places on the map when this session
# began (23, nearly all in Baker County). Claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest (region or). One or two places in each of
# Harney, Grant, Wasco, Linn, Josephine, Jackson, Union, Malheur, Lane, Multnomah and Clackamas counties, chosen from
# Register listings and state heritage sites: a round barn, a Chinese herb shop, a wool-boom ghost town, a stone pelota
# court, an 1858 water mill, a mining ghost town, an 1883 stage inn, an 1863 bank, a hot-spring sanatorium, a lighthouse,
# a gorge-top rest stop, an army surgeon's house and the house of the Hudson's Bay Company's chief factor.
# Facts from the linked sources only: Oregon State Parks park pages (and the agency's own park list, which gives each park's
# status note), the National Park Service, the Fort Dalles Museum, Historic Jacksonville, Inc., the Oregon Encyclopedia,
# a La Grande Observer article, and Wikipedia read with tools/wiki_raw.py.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
OP='https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=%s'
OE='https://oregonencyclopedia.org/articles/%s/'
BATCH={'key':'or_zoye','label':'Oregon, a first scatter','added':'2026-11-22 04:19'}
PLACES=[
N('pete-french-round-barn','Pete French Round Barn','Diamond, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',43.13258,-118.64256,'exact','public','drive','','1883 or 1884',
  'A round, roofed horse corral built by the cattleman Peter French and his P Ranch vaqueros in 1883 or 1884 (the Oregon Encyclopedia; Wikipedia says the late 1870s or early 1880s). French came to the Blitzen Valley in 1872 and by the mid-1880s ran 70,000 acres and 45,000 cattle; his land disputes ended in his murder in 1897. The barn was one of three on the ranch and the only one left; young horses and mules were broken and trained in it through the winter. The Jenkins family, who used it for storage from the early 1920s, gave it to the Oregon Historical Society, which gave it to the state parks in 1995 (Wikipedia gives 1969 for the gift to the state).',
  'The barn itself, about 100 feet across, on a low rise in the sagebrush. Outside, 29 peeled juniper posts carry the low conical shingle roof; fifteen feet in, a stone wall a foot and a half thick and about 9 feet high rings the 64-foot inner corral, with two gates and fourteen small windows, and a single 25-foot juniper pole stands at the centre with fifteen braces radiating from it. The covered track between the walls was the winter exercise ring. The pine lumber was hauled from a sawmill more than 36 miles north (Oregon Encyclopedia).',
  'A round barn of juniper poles and stone where a cattle king broke his horses.',
  'Pete French Round Barn State Heritage Site, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, on Round Barn Road, about 13 miles south of Malheur Lake and 50 miles southeast of Burns. A small lot by the barn. The park page gives no hours or fees (park information 541-932-4453).',
  [W('Pete French Round Barn'),['Oregon State Parks: Pete French Round Barn',OP%'209'],['Oregon Encyclopedia: Peter French Round Barn',OE%'peter_french_round_barn']],
  conf='mixed',th=[43.13259,-118.64304],thn='Round Barn lot, Round Barn Road',brick='no'),
N('frenchglen-hotel','Frenchglen Hotel','Frenchglen, Oregon','or','hotel','standing',42.82633,-118.91475,'site','open','drive','','about 1917 or 1924',
  'A small frame hotel at Frenchglen, at the foot of Steens Mountain, built for people with business at Peter French\'s old P Ranch and visitors to the Malheur bird refuge. Oregon State Parks says it was built about 1917; Wikipedia says 1924. The federal wildlife service restored and enlarged it in 1937 and 1938 with Civilian Conservation Corps labour, and teachers at the Frenchglen school boarded there. The state bought it from the General Services Administration in 1973, and it was listed on the National Register in 1984.',
  'A working country hotel: eight rustic rooms with the bathrooms down the hall, a lobby and a dining room where guests eat together, and the newer Drover\'s Inn on the same lot. Interior fittings from the 1930s work not checked.',
  'A CCC-restored hotel where guests still share the bathroom down the hall.',
  'Frenchglen Hotel State Heritage Site, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, on Oregon Route 205 in Frenchglen, at the north end of the Steens Mountain loop road. Rooms by reservation through the hotel\'s site; hours from the park page (park information 541-493-2825). Park along the street in front.',
  [W('Frenchglen Hotel State Heritage Site'),['Oregon State Parks: Frenchglen Hotel',OP%'2']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='March 15 to October 31: hotel and restaurant open; breakfast daily 7:30 to 9:30 a.m.; picnic lunch and family-style dinner by reservation'),
N('kam-wah-chung','Kam Wah Chung & Co.','John Day, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',44.41869,-118.95692,'exact','open','walk','','about 1865–1952',
  'A small building put up beside The Dalles-Canyon City wagon road, about 1865 by Wikipedia (around 1866 by Oregon State Parks), probably as a trading post or stage stop. In 1887 or 1888 two young Chinese immigrants from Canton, Ing "Doc" Hay and Lung On, took it over: Hay as an herbal doctor, Lung On as a merchant and letter-writer, and it was a store, clinic, temple and home for Chinese miners and travellers into the 1940s. After Hay died in 1952 it stood shut; the city of John Day found in 1967 that it owned it, and restored it as it had been in the 1940s. It was listed on the National Register in 1973 and made a National Historic Landmark in 2005.',
  'The building with its contents left as they were: the herb stock of the apothecary and the store, the shrine, and the living quarters, which Wikipedia calls one of the largest collections from Chinese immigration in the American West. Inside only by guided tour, eight people at a time.',
  'A Chinese doctor\'s shop shut in 1952 with every herb still on the shelf.',
  'Kam Wah Chung State Heritage Site, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, off NW Canton Street in John Day. Tours run on the hour and can be booked by calling the site. Where to park was not checked on the ground; the aerial shows street parking round the park.',
  [W('Kam Wah Chung & Co. Museum'),['Oregon State Parks: Kam Wah Chung',OP%'5']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='May 1 to October 31, daily: guided tours on the hour, first at 9 a.m., last at 4 p.m., eight people at most'),
N('shaniko-ghost-town','Shaniko and the Columbia Southern Hotel','Shaniko, Oregon','or','town','standing',45.00392,-120.75266,'site','public','drive','','1900–1966',
  'A railroad town on the high plateau, built where the Columbia Southern Railway ended in 1900. It was incorporated in 1902 and for a few years was a great wool market, with the largest wool warehouse in the state; in 1903 it shipped 2,229 tons of wool and called itself the Wool Capital of the World. A new line up the Deschutes canyon took its traffic from 1911, passenger trains stopped in the early 1930s and the branch was closed by 1966; by 1982 Shaniko was nearly empty. The railway built the Columbia Southern Hotel in 1900 to 1902, the biggest building in town. It was shut and reopened more than once; Wikipedia says it reopened to the public in August 2023.',
  'The Shaniko Historic District on the National Register: the hotel, the city hall and jail, a schoolhouse and over forty other buildings, some restored, with a wagon yard, a barn, the post office and a small museum. Wikipedia says the shops in town open seasonally, April to September. A 2009 photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows the hotel as a two-story red brick block with a long porch and balcony. The hotel\'s present state was not checked from its own page; interiors not checked.',
  'A wool capital of the world shrunk to 30 people.',
  'An incorporated town of about 30 people at U.S. Route 97 and Oregon Route 218, 8 miles north of Antelope. Walk the public streets; the buildings are privately owned; one businessman bought the hotel and several other properties in 2000 (Wikipedia). Park along the streets.',
  [W('Shaniko, Oregon'),W('Columbia Southern Hotel')],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('fort-dalles-surgeons-quarters','Fort Dalles Surgeon\'s Quarters (Fort Dalles Museum)','The Dalles, Oregon','or','fort','standing',45.59610,-121.19725,'exact','open','drive','','1856',
  'Fort Dalles began as an army post on the Columbia in 1850, first named Camp Drum, and took its name in 1853. From 1856 to 1858 its commander, Captain Thomas Jordan, put up new buildings at a cost of nearly $500,000. The surgeon\'s quarters of 1856, built during the Yakima wars, is the only officers\' house left of that fort (Wikipedia). It has held the Fort Dalles Museum since 1905, which makes it one of Oregon\'s oldest museum buildings, and it is on the National Register.',
  'The Gothic Revival officers\' house with pioneer and military collections inside, antique wagons, and an 1895 hand-hewn log farmhouse and barn set up as a homestead (museum page). Self-guided visits; group tours by reservation.',
  'The army doctor\'s house is all that is left of a half-million-dollar fort.',
  'Fort Dalles Museum, 500 W 15th Street in The Dalles, run by the museum. Admission $10 adults, $8 over 60, $5 children 5 to 17 (museum page). Free parking at the museum. The museum page says it is closed from November 22, 2026 to January 1, 2027.',
  [W('Fort Dalles'),['Fort Dalles Museum','https://fortdallesmuseum.org/']],
  brick='?',hours='March to November daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; January and February daily 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed November 22, 2026 to January 1, 2027'),
N('thompsons-mills','Thompson\'s Mills','Shedd, Oregon','or','industry','standing',44.46115,-123.08127,'exact','open','drive','','1858–2004',
  'A water-powered grain mill on the Calapooia River, first built in 1858; it burned in 1862 and was rebuilt at once. Its owners turned it from flour to feed and in the end to making electricity, and it ran until 2004, which made it one of the oldest water-powered businesses in Oregon. The state bought it that year and opened it as a park in December 2007. Oregon State Parks calls it the oldest standing water-powered grain mill in the state.',
  'Hand-hewn 12-inch beams of the 1862 rebuilding inside the mill, the water turbines and the milling machinery, which the guides run on water or electric power on most tours (park page; Wikipedia). The grounds are being put back to how they looked from 1910 to the 1930s, with an heirloom orchard and gardens.',
  'An 1862 mill whose turbines still turn for visitors.',
  'Thompson\'s Mills State Heritage Site, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, on Boston Mill Drive east of Shedd. Tours are free. Park in the lot south of the mill.',
  [W('Thompson\'s Mills State Heritage Site'),['Oregon State Parks: Thompson\'s Mills',OP%'186']],
  th=[44.46019,-123.08162],thn='Park lot, Boston Mill Drive',brick='no',hours='Daily except Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day: grounds dawn to dusk; mill 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., free guided and self-guided tours'),
N('golden-ghost-town-or','Golden','Golden, Oregon','or','town','standing',42.6846,-123.3394,'area','public','walk','','1850s–1920',
  'A gold camp on Coyote Creek. Miners came in the 1850s; Chinese miners later worked claims others had left. A town grew up about 1890, and by 1892 over 150 people lived along the creek. It was known for having no saloons, for its two churches and for the orchard of its founding families. A post office opened in 1896 and closed in 1920; a stamp mill went up in 1915. Josephine County bought the old Ruble mining claims in 1968; the town was listed on the National Register in 2002 and the state parks bought it that year.',
  'Four buildings: the church (rebuilt in 1950, by Wikipedia), a house, a shed, and the building that held the store and post office (Oregon State Parks). Wikipedia also names a carriage house. Interiors not checked.',
  'A mining town that kept its churches and never had a saloon.',
  'Golden State Heritage Site, owned by Oregon Parks and Recreation Department since 2002 (its park page; the federal land map does not show it), the only mining town the agency owns. Open for day use all year (park page; information 541-582-1118). On Coyote Creek Road east of Wolf Creek. Where to park was not checked: no lot is mapped at the buildings.',
  [W('Golden, Oregon'),['Oregon State Parks: Golden',OP%'189']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('wolf-creek-inn','Wolf Creek Inn','Wolf Creek, Oregon','or','hotel','standing',42.69516,-123.396,'exact','open','drive','','about 1883',
  'A stage inn built about 1883 for Henry Smith, a local merchant, on the Applegate Trail road between Roseburg and Redding; it served mine traffic and stage passengers until the railroad crossed the Siskiyous in 1887. Wikipedia calls it the oldest inn in the Pacific Northwest still in business. Jack London finished Valley of the Moon there, and Clark Gable stopped there in the 1930s. It was listed on the National Register as Wolf Creek Tavern in 1972, and the state bought and restored it between 1975 and 1979.',
  'The inn, still open with rooms and a restaurant that serves the public breakfast, lunch and dinner, and its grounds. Original fittings inside not checked.',
  'A stagecoach inn still feeding travellers after 140 years.',
  'Wolf Creek Inn State Heritage Site, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, on Old Highway 99 in Wolf Creek. The park page says the grounds are open dawn to dusk every day, free, and also gives the day-use season as May 1 to October 1. Park in the lot beside the inn.',
  [W('Wolf Creek Inn State Heritage Site'),['Oregon State Parks: Wolf Creek Inn',OP%'74']],
  brick='?',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk daily, free; restaurant open to the public for breakfast, lunch and dinner (days not given); day use May 1 to October 1'),
N('beekman-bank','Beekman Bank','Jacksonville, Oregon','or','civic','standing',42.31438,-122.96779,'exact','open','walk','','1856; building 1863–1915',
  'Cornelius Beekman began a gold-dust and express office in Jacksonville in 1856, the oldest financial house in the Pacific Northwest by Historic Jacksonville, Inc.; he became the town\'s Wells Fargo agent. The present bank building went up in 1863, and when Beekman closed the doors in 1915 it was left as it was. It is now owned by the city and stands in the Jacksonville Historic District, a National Historic Landmark since 1966 for the gold town the railroad passed by in 1884.',
  'The oldest bank building still standing in Oregon, with its original fixtures still inside (Wikipedia; Historic Jacksonville). In season costumed docents show it from behind the counter; evening lantern tours by reservation in the off-season. The rest of the historic district, including the 1880s courthouse, is around it.',
  'A gold-rush bank left exactly as it was the day it closed in 1915.',
  'At California and North 3rd streets in Jacksonville; owned by the city and opened by Historic Jacksonville, Inc., which asks a $3 "deposit". Park on the street or in the public lots on West C Street.',
  [W('Jacksonville Historic District (Jacksonville, Oregon)'),['Historic Jacksonville: Behind the Counter tours','https://www.historicjacksonville.org/?p=1849'],['Historic Jacksonville: Secrets and Mysteries of the Beekman Bank','https://historicjacksonville.org/beekman-bank-secrets/']],
  brick='no',hours='Late May to early September: Saturdays and Sundays 11 a.m. to 3 p.m.; evening lantern tours some second Saturdays, by reservation'),
N('hot-lake-hotel','Hot Lake Hotel','Hot Lake, Oregon','or','hotel','standing',45.24352,-117.95814,'exact','view','drive','','1864; 1907–1991',
  'A spa beside the largest hot spring in Oregon. A building of 1864 held a post office, smithy, dance hall and bathhouse; after the railroad came, a new hotel and sanatorium was begun in 1900 and opened in 1907, and Dr. William Phy, who bought it in 1917, made it a health resort called "the Mayo Clinic of the West". Wikipedia calls it the first commercial building known to be heated mainly by geothermal water. A fire in 1934 burned over half of it; it later served as a nursing school, from 1952 a nursing home, then a restaurant, and stood abandoned from 1991. The Manuel family restored it from 2003 (the west wing fell in 2008), and in April 2020 a neighbouring resort owner took it over and closed it for more renovation (La Grande Observer).',
  'The hotel buildings beside the lake, by Oregon Route 203. Wikipedia says 15 renovated suites opened in 2021. Whether it is open now was not checked: the resort\'s web address no longer belongs to it.',
  'Oregon\'s hot-spring "Mayo Clinic of the West", abandoned and brought back.',
  'Private. Look from Oregon Route 203 east of La Grande; go in only as a guest if it is open. The nearest public lot is the Ladd Marsh Wildlife Area lot on Peach Road, a mile north.',
  [W('Hot Lake Hotel'),['La Grande Observer: A new chapter for Hot Lake Springs (2020)','https://lagrandeobserver.com/?p=254650']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('pelota-fronton-jordan-valley','Pelota Fronton','Jordan Valley, Oregon','or','amuse','standing',42.97441,-117.0529,'exact','view','walk','','1915–1935',
  'A handball court built in 1915 by Basque sheepherders, who came to Jordan Valley between 1902 and 1916; Wikipedia says it was finished in 1917. Herders carried stone from a quarry east of town and dressed it by hand for the walls, and played blaid, the local form of pelota, with hard goatskin balls and wooden paddles. Immigration quotas in the 1920s and the Taylor Grazing Act of 1934, which took the open range away, cut the number of herders, and the court fell out of use after 1935. It was listed on the National Register in 1972 and restored in 1997.',
  'Two uneven stone walls 18 inches thick, buttressed outside: the front wall about 33 feet wide and 30 feet high, the side wall 93 feet long and stepping down towards the back, with lines marked on it and a strip across the front wall three feet above the concrete floor (Oregon Encyclopedia). A covered sign and a stone carving of the arms of the seven Basque provinces came with the 1997 restoration.',
  'A stone handball court that Basque sheepherders cut by hand.',
  'At U.S. Route 95 and Bassett Street in the middle of Jordan Valley. Who owns it was not checked: look from the street. Park along Bassett Street.',
  [W('Pelota Fronton'),['Oregon Encyclopedia: Pelota Fronton',OE%'pelota_fronton']],
  brick='no'),
N('heceta-head-lighthouse','Heceta Head Lighthouse','Florence, Oregon','or','light','standing',44.13737,-124.12784,'exact','open','walk','','1893–1894',
  'A 56-foot lighthouse halfway up a 205-foot headland, 13 miles north of Florence, first lit in 1894; Wikipedia calls its beam, seen 21 nautical miles out, the strongest on the Oregon coast. The light station was finished in August 1893. The head keeper\'s house was sold for $10 in 1940 and taken apart for lumber; the Queen Anne duplex of the two assistant keepers survives. The light was automated in 1963. Light and keepers\' house were listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'The tower with its Chance Brothers Fresnel lens, restored to look as it did in 1894; the ground floor is open, the upper levels are not. On the way up the trail, the 1893 assistant keepers\' house, one of the last on the Pacific coast, now a bed and breakfast run under the Forest Service.',
  'The strongest light on the Oregon coast, still turning its 1894 lens.',
  'Heceta Head Lighthouse State Scenic Viewpoint, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, off U.S. 101 north of Florence. A day-use parking permit is needed in the lot (bought on site); a half-mile trail climbs from the lot past the keepers\' house to the light. Information 541-547-3416.',
  [W('Heceta Head Light'),['Oregon State Parks: Heceta Head Lighthouse',OP%'86']],
  th=[44.13455,-124.12263],thn='Heceta Head day-use lot',brick='?',hours='Ground floor of the lighthouse open daily as weather and staffing permit; upper levels closed'),
N('vista-house-crown-point','Vista House','Corbett, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',45.53958,-122.2444,'exact','open','drive','','1916–1918',
  'An octagonal stone rest stop and observatory on Crown Point, 733 feet above the Columbia, where the Columbia River Highway was dedicated in 1916. Edgar M. Lazarus designed it and it opened in 1918; it cost about $70,000 for the building alone, and people at the time called it the "$100,000 outhouse". It is a memorial to the Oregon pioneers and was listed on the National Register in 1974. It was restored over five years from 2000.',
  'The stone octagon with its marble interior and brass fittings (Wikipedia), a gift shop and volunteers who give tours (Friends of Vista House); views up and down the gorge from the point.',
  'The "$100,000 outhouse" on a cliff above the Columbia.',
  'Vista House at Crown Point State Scenic Corridor, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department with the Friends of Vista House, on the Historic Columbia River Highway east of Corbett. A day-use parking permit is needed (bought on site); hours and weather closures are at vistahouse.com or 503-344-1368.',
  [W('Vista House'),['Oregon State Parks: Vista House',OP%'108']],
  th=[45.53860,-122.24476],thn='Crown Point lot, Historic Columbia River Highway',brick='?',hours='October 30, 2026 to March 21, 2027: Friday to Monday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; shut in winds over 50 mph; parking 6 a.m. to 9 p.m.'),
N('mcloughlin-house','McLoughlin House','Oregon City, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',45.35729,-122.60564,'exact','open','walk','','1846',
  'The house John McLoughlin settled into by Willamette Falls after he retired in 1846 as chief factor of the Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s Fort Vancouver, which he had run since 1825. He became an American citizen in 1851 and served as mayor of Oregon City. The McLoughlin Memorial Association saved the house from demolition and moved it to its present spot in 1909; it joined the National Park System in 2003 as part of Fort Vancouver National Historic Site.',
  'The house, shown by free tours that start at the Barclay House next door, the home of Dr. Forbes Barclay; park staff and volunteers also give talks and Victorian handcraft demonstrations (park page). Original furnishings not checked.',
  'The retirement house of the man who ran the Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s Pacific empire.',
  'National Park Service, at 713 Center Street in Oregon City; telephone 503-656-5151 while it is open. Tours are free and need no reservation. Park on the street or in the lots on Center Street.',
  [W('Fort Vancouver National Historic Site'),['National Park Service: McLoughlin House','https://www.nps.gov/fova/planyourvisit/placestogo-mcloughlin-house.htm']],
  brick='no',hours='Fridays and Saturdays 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; free tours on the hour from the Barclay House next door'),
]
